referenceSolutions.python:
def windowPegsPeakTicket(readings, k):
    a=readings
    out=[]
    from collections import deque
    queue=deque()
    for i,x in enumerate(a):
        while queue and queue[0]<=i-k: queue.popleft()
        while queue and a[queue[-1]]<=x: queue.pop()
        queue.append(i)
        if i>=k-1: out.append(a[queue[0]])
    return max(out,default=0)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int windowPegsPeakTicket(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++){while(!queue.isEmpty()&&queue.peekFirst()<=i-k)queue.removeFirst();while(!queue.isEmpty()&&a[queue.peekLast()]<=a[i])queue.removeLast();queue.addLast(i);if(i>=k-1)out.add(a[queue.peekFirst()]);}}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Take every group of exactly k numbers next to each other in readings. Move the group one place right each time. Record the largest number in each group, from left to right. If k is larger than the input length, the result list is empty.

Return the answer

Return the largest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0. If all numbers are negative, return the largest negative number; do not replace it with 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

